    "created_at": "2020-11-29T20:11:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-11-29T20:11:55Z",
    "body": "> I leave it to you how much you care about supporting the ancient bash version that ships with even modern Macs.\r\n\r\n@dunmatt We actually explicitly try to keep support for older bashes, specifically because we're one of the only shell frameworks that works for these older shells. (We have some users on Scientific Linux 6 as well).\r\n\r\nI did a little poking around, and while I can't exactly replicate your issue (maybe because Apple made some minor changes to bash between 10.14 and 11.0?), I found that mixing `/bin/bash` and `/usr/local/bin/bash` from homebrew does result in inconsistent behavior for SHLVL. You can see that when I switch between the two, SHLVL sometimes doesn't increment and sometimes jumps up by two:\r\n\r\n```\r\nThe default interactive shell is now zsh.\r\nTo update your account to use zsh, please run `chsh -s /bin/zsh`.\r\nFor more details, please visit https://support.apple.com/kb/HT208050.\r\n\r\n↕️  1 ~\r\n➜ /usr/local/bin/bash\r\n\r\n↕️  1 ~\r\n➜ /usr/local/bin/bash\r\n\r\n↕️  2 ~\r\n➜ /usr/local/bin/bash\r\n\r\n↕️  3 ~\r\n➜ /usr/local/bin/bash\r\n\r\n↕️  4 ~\r\n➜ exit\r\n\r\n↕️  3 ~ took 2s\r\n➜ exit\r\n\r\n↕️  2 ~ took 4s\r\n➜ exit\r\n\r\n↕️  1 ~ took 7s\r\n➜ exit\r\n\r\n↕️  1 ~ took 8s\r\n➜ /bin/bash\r\n\r\nThe default interactive shell is now zsh.\r\nTo update your account to use zsh, please run `chsh -s /bin/zsh`.\r\nFor more details, please visit https://support.apple.com/kb/HT208050.\r\n\r\n↕️  2 ~\r\n➜ /usr/local/bin/bash\r\n\r\n↕️  2 ~\r\n➜ /bin/bash\r\n\r\nThe default interactive shell is now zsh.\r\nTo update your account to use zsh, please run `chsh -s /bin/zsh`.\r\nFor more details, please visit https://support.apple.com/kb/HT208050.\r\n\r\n↕️  4 ~\r\n➜\r\n\r\n↕️  4 ~\r\n➜ /bin/bash --version\r\nGNU bash, version 3.2.57(1)-release (x86_64-apple-darwin20)\r\nCopyright (C) 2007 Free Software Foundation, Inc.\r\n\r\n↕️  4 ~\r\n➜\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo I'm suspecting that somehow, the way that bash 3.2 and bash 5.0 handle `SHLVL` is inconsistent.\r\n\r\nIf you start alacritty with /bin/bash and then explicitly spawn new copies of  `/bin/bash` (instead of whichever `bash` is picked up by your `PATH`), does `SHLVL` work? If so, I think the issue is that the two shells (3.2 and 5.0) are fundamentally incompatible as far as `SHLVL` goes, and I'm not sure we can do much on our end aside from warning people that `SHLVL` isn't consistent when mixing bash versions.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
